.TH CP 1
.SH NAME
cp, mv \- copy, move files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
.I file1 file2
.br
.B cp
.I file ... directory
.PP
.B mv
.I file1 file2
.br
.B mv
.I file ... directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
In the first form
.I file1
is any name and
.I file2
is any name except an existing directory.
In the second form the commands
copy or move one or more
.I files
into a
.I directory
under their original file names, as if by a sequence of
commands in the first form.
Thus
.B "cp f1 f2 dir"
is equivalent to
.BR "cp f1 dir/f1; cp f2 dir/f2" .
The second form is chosen when the last argument is an existing directory.
More than one source with a last argument that is not a directory is an error.
.PP
.I Cp
copies the contents of plain
.I file1
to
.IR file2 ,
in blocks of 8192 bytes.
If
.I file2
exists it is truncated and keeps its mode.
Otherwise it is created with mode 0666.
.PP
.I Mv
moves
.I file1
to
.IR file2 .
It reads all of
.I file1
into memory, writes it to
.IR file2 ,
and removes
.IR file1 .
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR mv ,
it never renames, because no file server here renames.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/cp/main.odin
.br
.B cmd/mv/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cat (1),
.IR rm (1),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is
.B usage
when the arguments are wrong,
.B "copy failed"
or
.B "move failed"
when any file could not be read, created or written,
and empty otherwise.
Each failure is reported on standard error and the rest of the files
are still copied.
.SH BUGS
Neither
.I cp
nor
.I mv
copies a directory.
Neither refuses to copy a file onto itself:
.B cp a a
empties
.B a
and
.B mv a a
removes it.
.I Mv
holds the whole file in memory, so a file larger than the heap cannot
be moved.
